		},
		{
			"type": "heading",
			"depth": 2,
			"children": [
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": "How does prefetching work?"
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"type": "paragraph",
			"children": [
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": "When navigating between routes, the browser requests assets for the page like HTML and JavaScript files. Prefetching is the process of fetching these resources "
				},
				{
					"type": "emphasis",
					"children": [
						{
							"type": "text",
							"value": "ahead"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": " of time, before you navigate to a new route."
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"type": "paragraph",
			"children": [
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": "Next.js automatically splits your application into smaller JavaScript chunks based on routes. Instead of loading all the code upfront like traditional SPAs, only the code needed for the current route is loaded. This reduces the initial load time while other parts of the app are loaded in the background. By the time you click the link, the resources for the new route have already been loaded into the browser cache."
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"type": "paragraph",
			"children": [
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": "When navigating to the new page, there's no full page reload or browser loading spinner. Instead, Next.js performs a "
				},
				{
					"type": "link",
					"title": null,
					"url": "../01-getting-started/04-linking-and-navigating.md#client-side-transitions",
					"children": [
						{
							"type": "text",
							"value": "client-side transition"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": ", making the page navigation feel instant."
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"type": "heading",
			"depth": 2,
			"children": [
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": "What Next.js prefetches automatically"
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"type": "paragraph",
			"children": [
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": "Next.js prefetches automatically in production. As each "
				},
				{
					"type": "inlineCode",
					"value": "<Link>"
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": " enters the viewport, Next.js prefetches the route behind it and schedules the work so a page full of links doesn't flood the network. How much of each route it prefetches depends on whether the route is static or dynamic, and changes when "
				},
				{
					"type": "link",
					"title": null,
					"url": "#partial-prefetching",
					"children": [
						{
							"type": "text",
							"value": "Partial Prefetching"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": " is enabled."
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"type": "heading",
			"depth": 3,
			"children": [
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": "Prefetching static vs. dynamic routes"
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"type": "paragraph",
			"children": [
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": "Without Cache Components, a static route is prefetched in full, while a dynamic route is skipped unless it has a "
				},
				{
					"type": "link",
					"title": null,
					"url": "../03-api-reference/03-file-conventions/loading.md",
					"children": [
						{
							"type": "inlineCode",
							"value": "loading.js"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"value": " boundary."
				}
			]
		},
